.TH "delay" 1G "5 October 2026" GINI "gRouter Commands"

.SH NAME
delay \- add latency and jitter to the router, like a long or busy link

.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
delay show
delay ingress | egress base_ms [jitter_ms [correlation [limit]]]
delay ingress | egress off
delay clear
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION

GINI links are instant. delay holds packets in a queue for a while before letting them go, so a
router can stand in for a slow link: a satellite hop, a congested path, a far-away server.

There are two delay lines per router, shared by all its interfaces.
.B ingress
holds every packet as it arrives, before the router looks at it.
.B egress
holds every packet just before it is sent out an interface, including the router's own replies.
A ping that crosses the router passes its egress line once each way, so
.B delay egress 20
adds about 40 ms to the round trip.

base_ms is the average delay. jitter_ms makes it vary: each packet's delay is spread around
base_ms by about jitter_ms (a standard deviation). correlation, from 0 up to (not including) 1,
makes the variation drift slowly instead of jumping from one packet to the next; 0 is
independent per packet. Packets always leave in the order they arrived, even with jitter.

limit is how many packets the line can hold at once (default 4096), like a link's buffer. When it
is full, new packets are dropped.

.B show
prints both lines with their settings and, for each, how many packets are held right now, how
many have passed and how many were dropped for a full buffer.
.B off
removes one line,
.B clear
removes both. Anything still held is dropped.

The Router Lab's Link delay tab sets the same two lines.

.SH EXAMPLES

A 50 ms link with 10 ms of jitter on the way out:
.RS
.nf
delay egress 50 10
delay show
.fi
.RE

Jitter that wanders rather than jumps, and a small buffer that overflows under load:
.RS
.nf
delay egress 50 10 0.8 100
.fi
.RE

Back to an instant router:
.RS
.nf
delay clear
.fi
.RE

.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR gpipe (1G),
.BR ping (1G),
.BR ifstat (1G)
